Z||000005||Motorhead||05-05-99||07:28 PM||||Hey Paul!<p>Yeah, I'd gathered from some of your posts that you had ditched the electronics in favor of a carb set-up. I'm an old multi-carb English car driver myself, I got really proficiant at tunin' and syncin' untill I tried to work on my wifes Fiat f'in Spyder! What a jolt! Aren't state of the art, and Italian Engineering a contradiction in terms?  Sorry, I digress!<p>Isn't there a cheap diagnostic "thingy" I can buy to plug into this car to make it divulge its ailements? I seem to remember reading somewhere about such a magical device. <p>Thanks for your help guys!
Z||000006||Patrick||05-05-99||07:46 PM||||Hi Motorhead,<p>Sorry to hear that my MAP sensor "trick" didn't work for you.<p>Regarding your inquiry about a cheap diagnostic "thingy", are you referring to the paper clip in the ALCL connector procedure? This is what you need to do to read the stored trouble codes. Do you have any idea what I'm talking about? If you're interested, just ask and one of us will explain it to you.<p><b>By the way, I hear that you miss your carburetors.</b> :)<p> 
Z||000007||Motorhead||05-06-99||02:51 AM||||You got it Patrick!<p>I've only picked up bits and pieces, but isn't the ALCL connector behind the cigarette lighter plate, and isn't there a procedure for retrieving the codes you mentioned?<p>Someone please enlighten me.
Z||000008||theogre||05-06-99||07:17 AM||||the ALDL is located under the trim plate for the lighter. To recover the codes jumper the a-b terminals, and turn the key to run. the first code will be 12. each code will flash out on the dash light 3 times. if there are no codes you will only see code 12 repeat contiuously. (the A-B terminals are on the bottom, farthest from the driver seat.)<p>You can also buy a "GM only" ECM scanner tool for around 150$us. This tool will allow much more information to be extracted from the ECM than simple code flashing. 
Z||000009||Patrick||05-06-99||02:32 PM||||Hi Motorhead,<p>I've got to say one very important thing before you start messing about retrieving trouble codes:<p><b>DON'T CRANK THE ENGINE WHILE YOU HAVE THE JUMPER WIRE INSERTED IN THE ALCL CONNECTOR!!!</b><p>Sorry for shouting, but I didn't want to see you possibly damage your ECM.:)<p>Anyone who wants to access the trouble codes for their Fiero would be well advised to get the Haynes Pontiac Fiero manual (or a similar publication). It explains the process, and also lists the codes. Retrieving the codes is pointless unless you know what the numbers represent.<p> 
