Z||000016||Motorhead||05-07-99||12:07 PM||||Thanks Ogre, I'll check it out.  Just out of curiosity, do you think this could be a coil problem?  Heat builds up in the compartment and causes ignition breakdown. I've had this problem before in the old English cars, old Lotus Europas had lots of heat problems,[mid-engine], but I'm still feeling my way along with these "electronic marvels " we own.
Z||000017||Cliff Pennock||05-07-99||04:11 PM||||Just my 2c:<p>If it were a problem with the Ignition coil/module, you would have problems during normal driving as well (misfires, stalling, etc.). If it drives fine but the problem only seems to be starting it while the engine is hot, I would look somewhere else.<p>Does the "Check Engine"/"Service Engine Soon" light come on when you turn the ignition? If it does and it doesn't light up while driving, there probably isn't a problem with a sensor and there will not be any ECM codes to read.<p>When you turn the ignition (without starting the engine), do you briefly hear the fuel pump (1 sec)?<br>
Z||000018||Motorhead||05-07-99||05:10 PM||||Called the Fiero Store today to see where my parts order is.  I think they lost it, repeat order on the way Priority Mail!  My Haynes manual is in that order,so I'm still waiting!!<p>Driving the car back and forth to work,[7 miles one way], it seems to have developed some hesitation and "misfiring " symptoms. This is what is leading me in the direction of ignition. After the car warms up to normal operating temperature, hesitation during acceleration becomes evident.<p>You've got to see this wiring harness to understand my scepticism about the electrical system, there is 16 gauge wire spliced into some interesting points in the harness, followed up with generous amounts of electrical tape to seal the job! Next project is to figure out why the previous owner decided to tap into the cooling fan harness. It has yet to operate!<p>Fuel Pump?  I'm used to hearing electric fuel pumps running for up to 3 or 4 seconds prior to start up.  I ain't heard a purr out of this car's fuel pump since I brought it back to life! A possibility!<p>Keep in mind, this car hasn't been driven much yet, it was abused severely in the maintenance department, but this isn't new to me. I've scraped alot of "SLUDGE" in my day!  The multiple cracks in the original head can, I'm sure, be traced back to an overheating condition. <p>I know I'm getting long winded, but, I've gone to great lengths to insure that this engine is in perfect operating condition, and this "GLITCH" is bugging the <br>s--- out of me!!<p>This car really looks great after the time and money spent on the restoration of the intirior and elbow grease exerted on the exterior,however, before going the full gambit on the suspension, I need to nail down this engine problem!<p>I love my Fiero to the point of talking to it and referring to it by name, but, if it doesn't respond to gentle stroking and carresing soon, I may have to resort to instigating jealousy through the open and blatant show of affection toward my Datsun!!<p><br>
Z||000019||Patrick||05-07-99||06:18 PM||||Hi Motorhead,<p>You mean to say that since you've gotten your engine back together that you've <b>never</b> heard your fuel pump? Not even when you turn the engine off when the engine is still cold? This is odd because I believe the fuel pump is set up in such a way that as long as there is <b>oil pressure</b>, the fuel pump will run. So when the engine is cold, and the <b>oil</b> is still thick and cold, it takes a few seconds for the oil pressure to drop enough for the fuel pump to power down. The reason why it's set up this way, is so that if there is an oil pump failure, the fuel pump will stop and the engine will stall <b>before</b> (hopefully) the engine seizes up.<p>Maybe you just can't hear the fuel pump any more because you've simply lost too much of your hearing from attending all those rock concerts as a youth.:)<p> 
